pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨17\\cos(t),\\,-17\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}17\\cos(t)\\cdot 17\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}289\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b85814f-cef1-50ce-a0c2-e193caf5926b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522940} {:answer/id #uuid "4de1dc13-89bc-5d8f-ac26-038f1d188087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 24 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522951} {:answer/id #uuid "7763e82b-d4bc-51ac-a9b0-38a352a32d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522968} {:answer/id #uuid "a8b48ef2-128e-5882-aeb3-90e0fe45e532" :answer/type