\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} & \\le -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} & \\le -\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\le \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{17}{6} & \\le y\\le -\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{17}{6}\\le y\\le -\\frac{11}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9047927d-1b44-5d57-a625-83428018d3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9047927d-1b44-5d57-a625-83428018d3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 