stion/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & \\le -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta -\\frac{3}{4}\\le \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le -\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{7}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{19}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{19}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{23}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee293cc5-69a9-52fb-997c-1b6841614969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee293cc5-69a9-52fb-997c-1b6841614969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526289} :answer-field/id #uuid "2042e91b-9fc0-5244-b482-8c216903850a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/bla